===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{senseid|en|speak or utter (words)}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[speak]]; to [[utter]].
#: {{ux|en|He '''mouthed''' his opinions on the subject at the meeting.}}
#* {{RQ:Hare Guesses|passage='''mouthing''' big phrases}}
# {{senseid|en|represent (words or sounds) orally but soundlessly}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To represent (words or sounds) by making the actions of [[speech]], but without producing sound.
#: {{cot|en|lip-sync#Verb|lip sync#Verb|mime}}
#: {{ux|en|The prompter '''mouthed''' the words to the actor, who had forgotten them.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010| title=Amazing Minds|author=Jan Faull; Jennifer McLean Oliver | page=| passage=One was of a face repeatedly '''mouthing''' the vowel sound eee, the other was of a face repeatedly '''mouthing''' the vowel sound ahhh.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2016| title=Theology without Words: Theology in the Deaf Community|author=Wayne Morris | page=| passage=It should be explained that lip patterns are generally not the same as patterns which would be made by ''''mouthing'''' words.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2020 | title=Maid to the Mafia|author=Wanitta Praks | page=| passage="So very hot,” I '''mouthed''' at myself in the mirror.}}
# {{senseid|en|move the mouth or form (words) with the mouth, with or without sound}} {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To move the mouth, with or without sound; to form (air or words) with the mouth, with or without sound.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1886| title=Polmood series |author=James Hogg | page=51| passage=But words are nothing to the misbelieving -- mere air '''mouthed''' into a sound.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1987| title=The Development of Behavioral States and the Expression of Emotions in Early Infancy|author=Peter H. Wolff | page=15| passage=There was also a close temporal contiguity between "smiling" or other "emotional" grimaces and '''mouthing''' and tonguing movements, so that it was often difficult to distinguish between '''mouthing''' and smiling.}}
# {{senseid|en|utter in a loud discordant voice}} {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To utter with a voice that is overly loud or swelling.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1846| title=Practical Speaking: As Taught in Yale College |author=Erasmus Darwin North | page=123| passage=Those who endeavor to become eloquent by mere imitation of some celebrated model—an actor for instance—often attempt to gain this quality by altering their voice in an unnatural manner. Such a process never produces any thing but '''mouthing'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1902| title=The Practical treatment of stammering and stuttering |author=George Andrew Lewis | page=195| passage=This view of voice cultivation excludes all '''mouthing''' and ranting which have been thought to be necessary incidents of voice culture. }}
# To exit at a mouth (such as a river mouth)
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1906| title=Philosophical Magazine |author=| page=96| passage=In this part of the address the position of the principal hanging-valleys was indicated , and it was pointed out that there were two sets, namely those which '''mouthed''' into valleys that had been deepened in softer rocks, and those which  '''mouthed''' into portions of main valleys that had been deepened along shatter-bolts.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999 | title=Qualla: Home of the Middle Cherokee Settlement |author=T. Walter Middleton | page=39| passage=Suddenly an avalanche of stones turned loose right down a ravine and '''mouthed''' out on the road, stones large enough to knock a horse down, or larger, and a plenty of them to do a fair job on a large group.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[pick up]] or [[handle]] with the [[lips]] or mouth, but not [[chew]] or [[swallow]].
#: {{ux|en|The fish '''mouthed''' the lure, but didn't bite.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1887|month=September|work=Hardwicke's Science-gossip|title=Natural History Jottings: On Wasps, chiefly|author=Charles Robson|editor=Mordecai Cubitt Cooke; John Eller Taylor |page=210|number=273|passage=She alighted and '''mouthed''' over several within a small space and a short time; and these buds were not at the bottom of the hedge; nor was she searching for a nest-site.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1889| title=The Cruise of the Marchesa to Kamschatka & New Guinea |author=Francis Henry Hill Guillemard | page=165| passage=His manner of feeding was curious, any fish he was provided with not being snapped up immediately, but played with and '''mouthed''' all over for a quarter of an hour or more, when it suddenly disappeared as if by magic.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1920| title=The Dreadful River Cave: Chief Black Elk's Story |author=James Willard Schultz | page=50| passage=He would not touch any of our food that the bears had pawed and '''mouthed''' over, fearing it might be bad medicine for him, so some was got for him from Red Wing Woman.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1973|author=w:Thomas Pynchon| title=w:Gravity's Rainbow| passage= Everyone is kind of aroused, Thanatz is sitting up on the bar having his own as yet unsheathed penis '''mouthed''' by one of the white-gloved Wends.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999 | title=Sagebrush Homesteads |author=Laura Tice Lage | page=257| passage=She found foamy saliva where the coyote had '''mouthed''' over the dogs, but no place showed any bite. }}
# To take into the mouth; to seize or grind with the mouth or teeth; to chew; to devour.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1938| title=Seven Shifts |author=Jack Common | page=62| passage=Sometimes I ate food that the rats had already '''mouthed''' over; picking away the edges where they had been eating and using the remainder; not with any good grace, not without qualms; but because I had nothing else to eat. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998| title=Word of Mouth: A Manhattan Dentist Tells All-- (well, Almost) |author=Marvin K. Rubin | page=77| passage=Each contained a long, wide, solid oak table around which all who could find a space the width of his body would '''mouth''' his brown-bag grub from home. }}
# To form or cleanse with the mouth; to lick, as a bear licks her cub.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1915| title=Bulletin of the Bureau of Fisheries - Volume 33 |author=| page=224| passage=They were sucking off whatever adhered to the floating stems and leaves of the plants. They went from plant to plant and '''mouthed''' over each branch from base to tip until the whole plant had been gone over.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1937| title=Aquarium Journal - Volume 10, Issue 5 |author=| page=7| passage=Meanwhile she, and the male, '''mouthed''' over the inner surface of pot until it was as clean as could be.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1965| title=The Aquarist and Pondkeeper - Volumes 30-31 |author=| page=182| passage=Before egg-laying begins, the spawning grounds are '''mouthed''' over (cleaned) by both sexes.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1970| title=The Hoopoe |author=Christine Weston | page=6| passage=Small slobs of things, wet and sticky, which Peggy herself distastefully '''mouthed''' out from her cavity, biting the cord which bound them to her, swallowing it, then licking the puppies clean one by one.}}
# To carry in the mouth.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1953 | title=Keeping and Breeding Aquarium Fishes |author=Clifford Walter Emmens | page=154| passage=This transfer system continues until the young are free-swimming, which may be for another 3 or 4 days. Each time they are moved to a new pit, they are '''mouthed''' over and spat into their new crèche.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[make mouths at]]
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1827| title=Vivian Grey|author=Benjamin Disraeli| page=| passage=He drew the cork from his bottle..and '''mouthed''' at his companions even while he bowed to them. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1854| title=Poems of Thomas Hood|chapter=The Irish Schoolmaster|author=Thomas Hood| page=40|volume=2| passage=Meanwhile, the tyrant, with untimely wit And '''mouthing''' face, derides the small one's moan, Who, all lamenting for his loss, doth sit,}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1873| title=A Wonderful Woman|author=May Agnes Fleming | page=269| passage=And that other face -- that awful, gibbering, '''mouthing''' face she drew away. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1883| title=My Reminiscences - Volume 1 |author=Lord Ronald Sutherland Gower | page=148| passage=But active as this old professor of the dance was, he had when a child in Paris, in 1793, seen Marie Antoinette on the way to the scaffold, and described the unfortunate queen, with her gray hair cut short, her hands tied, seated in the cart, still retaining her calm demeanour as the mob shouted and '''mouthed''' around her .}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1906| title=At a Winter's Fire |author=Bernard Edward Joseph Capes | page=240| passage=The man '''mouthed''' at the bars. He was half-mad, I think, in that one minute. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014| title=Tied|author=Emma Chase | page=| passage=What ensues next is a comical soundless argument only true parents will understand. It's all '''mouthing''' and miming, facial expression and hand flailing.}}
# To form a mouth or [[opening]] in.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1773| title=Amateur work, illustrated - Volume 1|chapter=Lathe-Making For Amateurs |author=Paul. N. Hasluck| page=426| passage=The front end of the barrel has to be '''mouthed''' out conically, so that the various centre points may fit it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1882| title=The Metal Turner's Handbook|author=Paul Nooncree Hasluck | page=76| passage=The front collar must be '''mouthed''' out as shown, to take the second cone on the mandrel.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1956| title=Mechanical World and Engineering Record - Volume 136 |author=| page=471| passage=The cutting edge of a shaving cutter should be '''mouthed''' out slightly with a fine oilstone.}}
# {{lb|en|sheep husbandry}} To examine the [[teeth]] of.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1938| title=Type of Farming and Ranching Areas in Wyoming|author=Byron Hunter; Harry W. Pearson; Alonzo Frederick Vass | page=96| passage=Either at the shipping point or as they leave the summer range, the older ewes are “'''mouthed''' out.” That is, their mouths are examined to see if their teeth are good for another year.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1957| title=The New Zealand Journal of Science and Technology|author=| page=587| passage=No information could be found on the relationship between the productivity of ewes and the states of their mouths. While there is no doubt that the practice of “'''mouthing'''" ewes is founded on experience, the traditional standards may require modification since the adoption almost exclusively of grassland farming, particularly in the North Island.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1976 | title=Oregon Historical Quarterly |author=Oregon Historical Society | page=27| passage=After we got the ewes "'''mouthed''' out," we turned them over to the herder that Foncy had hired to drive them to Shaniko. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1977| title=Agriculture Decisions|author=United States. Department of Agriculture | page=1141| passage=Daniels told complainants in substance he would not buy any sheep without '''mouthing''' them.}}

